# ai.env — Configuration for ai.sh
# Uncomment and edit to override defaults.

# AI_DIR — Base directory (default: auto-detected from ai.sh location)
# AI_DIR="/Users/p/Development/tools/ai"

# AI_LOG_DIR — Server log directory (default: $AI_DIR/logs)
# AI_LOG_DIR="${AI_DIR}/logs"

# AI_STATE_DIR — State and PID files (default: ~/.local/state)
# AI_STATE_DIR="${HOME}/.local/state"

# AI_PORT — oMLX server port (default: 10081)
# AI_PORT=10081

# ── oMLX server ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One oMLX build serves every profile, so each of these applies to all four
# models at once.
#
# OMLX_BIN — oMLX binary (default: $OMLX_VENV/bin/omlx, else `omlx` on PATH)
# OMLX_BIN="${HOME}/.omlx/venv/bin/omlx"
# OMLX_VENV — venv holding that binary (default: ~/.omlx/venv)
# OMLX_VENV="${HOME}/.omlx/venv"
# OMLX_SRC_DIR — oMLX source checkout (default: ~/.omlx/src). The install is
#   editable, so this checkout's git HEAD is the code being served: ai.sh records
#   it in the state file, and a change there restarts a running server. An
#   in-place upgrade is invisible otherwise, because the binary path never moves.
# OMLX_SRC_DIR="${HOME}/.omlx/src"
# OMLX_MODEL_DIR — dir oMLX discovers models from (default: ~/.omlx/models).
#   Holds bge-m3 plus one symlink per roster model into LM Studio's store. Do NOT
#   repoint it at that store: bge-m3 lives here and would be stranded.
# OMLX_MODEL_DIR="${HOME}/.omlx/models"
# HF_HUB_CACHE — LM Studio's weight store, the single copy of every model's
#   weights (default: ~/.cache/huggingface/hub). ai.sh only ever reads and links
#   it; models are downloaded by hand in LM Studio.
# HF_HUB_CACHE="${HOME}/.cache/huggingface/hub"
# OMLX_BASE_DIR — oMLX's own base path (default: ~/.omlx). Only used to locate
#   $OMLX_BASE_DIR/model_settings.json, where scripts/patch-omlx-mtp.mjs writes the
#   per-model max_context_window rails. oMLX reads that file at MODEL LOAD, so a
#   change needs a server restart. Note the patch never DELETES a key: reverting a
#   pin means editing the file by hand.
# OMLX_BASE_DIR="${HOME}/.omlx"
# OMLX_CACHE_DIR — paged SSD KV-cache dir (default: ~/.omlx/cache)
# OMLX_CACHE_DIR="${HOME}/.omlx/cache"
# OMLX_HOT_CACHE — in-RAM hot KV tier size (default: 8GB; model + tier must
#   fit under the memory guard's soft threshold or oMLX evicts models mid-request)
# OMLX_HOT_CACHE="8GB"
# OMLX_SSD_CACHE_MAX — paged SSD cache disk cap (default: 25GB; unset, oMLX
#   claims nearly all free disk). ONE budget for the whole roster, so four profiles
#   compete for it. In blocks rather than bytes: ~89 blocks, about 182k tokens of
#   prefix, and a ~20k session costs ~10 blocks. oMLX enforces this itself, ACROSS
#   models — a departed model's blocks are indexed, counted and evicted first.
#   25 GB stays because no measurement moves it, not because it is optimal: hit
#   rate is unmeasured at any budget.
# OMLX_SSD_CACHE_MAX="25GB"
# OMLX_CACHE_PRUNE_GB — prune the on-disk KV cache to this many GB at each server
#   (re)start, oldest-first (default: numeric part of OMLX_SSD_CACHE_MAX, so it can
#   never disagree with what the server is told). This is a BACKSTOP for one case
#   oMLX's own LRU cannot reach: a block whose omlx_cache_format_version falls
#   outside the readable set enters no index and is never evicted, and the editable
#   oMLX checkout can bump that version on any pull. It bounds growth — it does not
#   free disk on demand, because macOS may hold the deleted space in APFS
#   snapshots. Set 0 to disable.
# OMLX_CACHE_PRUNE_GB=25
# OMLX_MEMORY_GUARD_GB — memory ceiling in GB (default: 48; raise on 128 GB)
# OMLX_MEMORY_GUARD_GB=48
# OMLX_MAX_CONCURRENT — max concurrent requests / continuous batching (default: 2 —
#   one coding turn + the opencode-mem summarizer; don't set to 1 or memory
#   captures serialize with coding turns)
# OMLX_MAX_CONCURRENT=2

# ── MTP speculative decode on --qwen (OFF — under measurement) ─────────
# Attaches the external mlx-community/Qwen3.8-27B-MTP-4bit drafter (239 MB) to
# --qwen. It ENGAGES: verified on every request including tool calls on both
# lanes, at 64-85% acceptance and 2.29-2.71 tokens per round, costing ~0.47 GB
# resident. But NO SPEED HAS BEEN MEASURED yet, and tokens_per_round is an upper
# bound on a speedup rather than one — each round pays a drafter forward plus a
# target verify. It stays off until the decision rule in
# .wayfinder/perf-headroom/tickets/02-native-mtp-qwen.md fires.
#
# A change here needs a SERVER RESTART. It does not move the build id, so ai.sh
# will not restart a running server for it.
#
# These two names are the only ones in this file read by a CHILD PROCESS (the node
# settings patch) rather than by ai.sh itself, so ai.sh marks them for export before
# it runs that patch. Without that, a plain assignment here would be a shell variable
# the patch never sees, and the opt-in would be a silent no-op.
# AI_QWEN_MTP=1
#
# Draft depth, and it does nothing while AI_QWEN_MTP is off. Unset, oMLX reads the
# drafter's own configured 3 — 2 drafted tokens plus 1 bonus. The head chains one
# MTP layer autoregressively, so a deeper block is only more drafter forwards, and
# this drafter class honours the requested number directly rather than treating it
# as a ceiling. Deeper is NOT automatically faster: every drafted token is paid for
# whether the verify accepts it or not. Sweep it, never guess it.
# AI_QWEN_MTP_DEPTH=5

# ── Prompt-prefix warm-up (OFF by default — it lost a measurement) ─────
# opencode sends the system prompt, AGENTS.md, the skill list and every tool
# definition BEFORE your message — ~13,400 tokens in this repo, even for "hi".
# The warm-up prefills that through `opencode run` (max_tokens 1, no decode)
# as soon as the server is ready. It is OFF because oMLX has no request
# priority: on a cold server the warm-up and your first turn contend, and one
# real launch turned a ~90 s answer into 2 m 14 s. Turn it on only if you
# launch `ai` and then leave it alone for a minute.
# AI_WARM_PREFIX — 1 enables the warm-up (default: 0)
# AI_WARM_PREFIX=0
# AI_WARM_PREFIX_MCP — 0 drops MCP tools from the warm-up. Do not: Muse's
#   template lists tool NAMES before the schemas, so the prefix then diverges
#   early (reused 6,144 of 13,324) and the box does 43 % more work than with
#   no warm-up at all. Kept only to reproduce that measurement.
# AI_WARM_PREFIX_MCP=1

# ── opencode-mem privacy: never store/recall memory from these trees ──
# Colon-separated dir prefixes. List your sensitive client-repo roots here so NO
# session in them touches the memory store.
# OPENCODE_MEM_EXCLUDE_DIRS="/Users/p/Development/client-acme:/Users/p/work/secret"

# OPENCODE_MEM_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS — char budget the auto-capture summarizer input is
#   capped to (default: 24000, about 6k tokens; read by the patched plugin, see
#   scripts/patch-opencode-mem-cap.mjs). The summarizer is a raw OpenAI client to
#   oMLX, so it bypasses opencode.json's context limit; unbounded it fed ~120k-token
#   prefills that saturated the memory guard and 507'd concurrent embedding loads.
#   It is a MEMORY guard, not a latency lever. Lowering it does NOT make a capture
#   finish sooner: the summarizer's wall is dominated by decode, and Muse Glimmer
#   reasons MORE when given less context — measured 44 s at 24000 chars against
#   76 s at 2000. Measured on Muse, which was the default then and is --muse now;
#   NO capture has been timed on any current profile — not on Bonsai, the default
#   since 2026-08-14, and not on Qwen3.8. See CLAUDE.md (Memory) and W20.
# OPENCODE_MEM_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS=24000
#
# (OPENCODE_MEM_MODEL is NOT set here — ai.sh sets it to the session's own model on
# every launch, so the summarizer reuses the resident model instead of loading a
# second one beside it.)

# ── smart-coding RAG ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SMART_CODING_OMLX_DIR — npm prefix holding the PRIVATE repo-owned copy of
#   smart-coding-mcp (default: ~/.smart-coding-omlx). It is private so ai.sh can
#   patch it — embedder rerouted to oMLX bge-m3, plus the exclude override upstream
#   lacks — without mutating a shared global package. opencode.json launches the
#   binary from this path, so changing it here alone is not enough.
# SMART_CODING_OMLX_DIR="${HOME}/.smart-coding-omlx"

# ── smart-coding RAG: extra directories to keep out of the index ──
# Colon-separated globs, appended to what ProjectDetector emits (see
# scripts/patch-smart-coding-excludes.mjs). Python virtualenv and tool-cache trees
# are already excluded by that patch's built-in set, so this is only for repo-
# specific noise.
#
# Each entry MUST be exactly "**/<dirname>/**" with no wildcard inside <dirname>:
# the indexer reduces every pattern to a bare directory name and exact-matches it
# per directory, so file-level globs like "**/*.min.js" silently do nothing.
#
# Worth considering: i18n locale dumps. Flat translation JSON carries no code
# semantics but chunks heavily — four locale files in one repo produced ~1,290
# chunks, re-embedded on every translation edit (watchFiles is on).
# SMART_CODING_EXCLUDE_PATTERNS="**/locales/**:**/fixtures/**:**/__snapshots__/**"

# Set to "false" to drop the built-in virtualenv/tool-cache excludes entirely.
# SMART_CODING_EXCLUDE_DEFAULTS="false"

# ── post-edit-check plugin (ESLint + tsc + Prettier, enforced on every edit) ──
# Read by plugins/post-edit-check.js, not by ai.sh. The plugin throws remaining
# errors back into the agent loop, so these are the only ways to soften it.
# NOTE: it covers JS/TS only — nothing here checks Bash, the language ai.sh is
# written in. Review Bash edits by hand.
#
# OPENCODE_LINT_ENABLED="true"
# OPENCODE_LINT_CHECKS="eslint,tsc,prettier"
# OPENCODE_LINT_MAX_RETRIES — consecutive blocking throws for the same file+error
#   set before the plugin warns instead of blocking (default: 3). This is the real
#   doom-loop guard: opencode.json sets "doom_loop": "allow".
# OPENCODE_LINT_MAX_RETRIES=3
# OPENCODE_LINT_EXTENSIONS=".ts,.tsx,.js,.jsx,.mjs,.cjs"
